OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} retains OpenVLA's MLP connector but replaces the action head; its attention entropy (0.667) matches standard OpenVLA (0.661), confirming the connector, not the action head, determines token importance structure. EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla} corroborates the graded pattern: CogACT~\cite{li2024cogact} (MLP projector) retains 74.2\% SR under 78\% pruning.

To control for scoring method in cross-architecture comparisons, we evaluate L2 norm-based selection on OpenVLA. At moderate-to-aggressive retention ($k \leq 0.7$), L2 performs within 2\,pp of random removal (Table~\ref{tab:openvla_l2_vs_random}, Appendix~\ref{app:l2_vs_random}), confirming that the cross-architecture comparison is not confounded by the choice of scoring criterion. At $k{=}0.9$, L2 outperforms random by 8.5\,pp (70.6\% vs.\ 62.0\%, reproducible across two random seeds), consistent with the weak but non-zero importance gradient predicted by CV$\,{=}\,$0.215: the signal is exploitable only when few tokens are removed.

